" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1033 1499 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186050886 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "6e409d61-3310-5c51-abad-da7413b800ab" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048427 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1027 #edb/ref 17592186048464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208728 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208729 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208730 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1028 #edb/ref 17592186208731 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1031 "Volumes of Cylinders" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1033 6028 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186051011 #edb/ref 1035 #uuid "79559ecc-1ed0-52de-9d1a-558d45add052" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151899 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21450" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151899 #edb/ref 1071 "What number is $40\\%$ greater than $360?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151899 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2cfd1ed7-8249-5970-9ddc-1bb69929951c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151899 #edb/ref 1074 "To find a number $40\\%$ *greater than* $360,$ we apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 40\\% = 140\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n140\\% = \\dfrac{140}{100} = 1.40\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $40\\%$ greater than $360,$ we multiply $360$ by $1.40{:}$\n\n$$\n1.40 \\cdot 360 = 504\n$$\n\nTherefore, the number that is $40\\%$ greater than $360$ is $504.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151900 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21451" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151900 #edb/ref 1071 "A t-shirt costs $\\[math]18$ before tax. If a $9\\%$ sales tax is applied, what is the total cost after tax?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151900 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "95c3dedd-accd-57dc-91ee-4ee8fdb2f3a4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151900 #edb/ref 1074 "To find the total cost after tax, we apply a $9\\%$ increase to the original price.\n\nIncreasing by $9\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 9\\% = 109\\% = 1.09\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original price by $1.09{:}$\n\n$$\n18 \\times 1.09 = 19.62.\n$$\n\nSo, the total cost after tax is $\\[math]19.62.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151901 #edb/ref 1069 "e-21517" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151901 #edb/ref 1071 "A number $x$ is $150\\%$ greater than $20.$ Find the value of $x.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151901 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4af9bc31-4980-5c3c-9025-45e85ac09d8a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151901 #edb/ref 1074 "To find a number $150\\%$ *greater than* $20,$ we apply a new percentage of  \n\n$$\n100\\% + 150\\% = 250\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting our percentage to a decimal:  \n\n$$\n250\\% = \\dfrac{250}{100} = 2.5\n$$\n\nSo, to find a number that is $150\\%$ greater than $20,$ we multiply $20$ by $2.5{:}$  \n\n$$\n2.5 \\cdot 20 = 50\n$$\n\nTherefore, $x = {50}.$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151913 #edb/ref 1069 "e-22093" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151913 #edb/ref 1071 "A gym had $80$ members last year. This year, membership is $400\\%$ greater than last year. How many members does the gym have this year?" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151913 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "92d9c3ca-6e59-5c73-b640-114dfb7d13cd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186151913 #edb/ref 1074 "To find this year's gym membership, we apply a $400\\%$ increase to last year's figure.\n\nIncreasing by $400\\%$ means that we must apply a new percentage of\n\n$$\n100\\% + 400\\% = 500\\%.\n$$\n\nWe begin by converting the percentage to a decimal:\n\n$$\n500\\% = \\dfrac{500}{100} = 5\n$$\n\nNow, we multiply the original amount by $5{:}$\n\n$$\n80 \\times 5 = 400\n$$\n\nSo, the gym has $\\bbox[4pt, border: 1px solid lightgray]{400}$ members this year." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186152295 #edb/ref 1037 #edb/ref 17592186050716 #edb/ref 175